All ten detected inactive asteroids in the GCN field show inactive states and rotation rates that are accelerated, with frequency changes ($\Delta 
u$) and second rate of change ($\Delta \dot{
u}$) of approximately $10^{-9}$ and $10^{-17}$, respectively. The observed frequency changes are attributed to the rotation of these asteroids, with the second rate of change indicating the acceleration of their rotation. The $\Delta \dot{
u}$ values suggest the rotation axis of the detected asteroids is close to our line of sight, with estimated obliquation angles of less than 20 degrees. Based on $\Delta \dot{
u}$, the acceleration of rotation can be calculated, with values of approximately $10^{20} cm s^{-2}$, consistent with the thrust produced by the Outburst of GCN. The detected asteroids exhibit frequency changes and obliquation angles that are expected, with the acceleration of their rotation aligning with the thrust produced by the Outburst, thus GCN is confirmed to be the origin of the GCN outburst.
